 # 3D Gaussian Splatting
 
-Also called: 3DGS
+Also called: 3D-GS rendering, 3DGS, Bernoulli sampling, R rasterization-based radiance field rendering, Rasterization-based radiance field rendering, Splat Regression Instance
 
 ## Overview
-3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) is a novel view synthesis and 3D reconstruction method that represents a scene as a collection of anisotropic 3D Gaussian primitives, each parameterized by position, covariance, opacity, and view-dependent color [S1][S6]. It solves the trade-off between the high-quality but slow volumetric rendering of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and the geometric limitations of mesh-based rendering by using explicit, differentiable particle-based discretization [S6][S18, unofficial]. Unlike NeRF, which requires expensive ray marching through a continuous MLP, 3DGS projects 3D Gaussians to 2D screen-space ellipses and rasterizes them using tile-based alpha compositing, enabling real-time rendering speeds (>100 FPS) and rapid training convergence [S6][S18, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [3D Gaussian Splatting Rendering (CPU-Sorted, Instanced)](2058-3d-gaussian-splatting-rendering-cpu-sorted-instanced.md) by utilizing a highly optimized, parallelized GPU rasterization pipeline with global depth sorting and tile-based compositing [S6][S12].
+3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) is a novel view synthesis method that represents a 3D scene as an explicit collection of anisotropic 3D Gaussian primitives, each defined by position, covariance, opacity, and view-dependent color [S1, S11, S15]. Unlike implicit neural radiance fields (NeRFs) that require volumetric ray marching, 3DGS projects these 3D Gaussians into 2D screen-space ellipses and renders them via a tile-based, differentiable rasterization pipeline with alpha compositing [S9, S15, S22]. This approach achieves real-time rendering speeds (100+ FPS) and significantly faster training times compared to NeRFs, while maintaining high photorealistic quality [S8, S27, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by using explicit, differentiable Gaussian primitives that allow for fast, tile-based rasterization and direct gradient flow to scene parameters, enabling end-to-end optimization from sparse 2D images [S14, S22].
 
